/* Real-world NFL career leaders by position, bounded to the 2002-2025 seasons
   — the span of MFFL's existence (2026 draft only just happened, so 2025 is
   the last completed season). This is trivia/reference content, NOT derived
   from the league archive and NOT tied to who any MFFL manager drafted.
   Intentionally separate from league.ts, same pattern as hallOfFame.ts.

   THE TOP 10 (TOP 5 FOR DEFENSE) IN EACH TABLE ARE RANKED BY TOTAL ESTIMATED
   FANTASY POINTS, not by any single raw counting stat. This matters: a
   high-volume pass-catcher or dual-threat player who doesn't lead a raw
   yardage column can still out-score everyone once receptions and rushing
   are folded in. Two players who only cracked these lists because of that:
   Christian McCaffrey (RB) and Russell Wilson (QB) — neither leads their
   position in yardage over 2002-2025, but both rank top-10 in estimated
   fantasy points once full-PPR receiving and rushing are counted.

   Sourced 2026-08-31 via StatMuse (official NFL play-by-play data),
   cross-checked against Pro-Football-Reference career spans for players
   whose careers start before 2002 or continue past 2025. Regular season
   only (playoffs excluded). Where a player's real career extends outside
   the window, only the portion played within 2002-2025 is counted.

   "Est. Fantasy Pts" uses MFFL's CURRENT (2026) scoring settings, pulled
   directly from the league's live Scoring & Settings page, applied
   uniformly across the whole 2002-2025 window at Marc's explicit direction
   — even though MFFL's real settings have changed over the league's
   history:
     Passing:   1 pt / 25 yds, 6 pts / TD, -2 pts / INT
     Rushing:   1 pt / 10 yds, 6 pts / TD
     Receiving: 1 pt / 10 yds, 6 pts / TD, 1 pt / reception (full PPR)
     K:         3 pts / FG made (see kicker note below), 1 pt / XP made
     DEF:       1 pt / sack, 2 pts / INT, 2 pts / fumble recovery,
                6 pts / defensive-or-return TD

   Left out for lack of per-game public data at the career-total level
   (all real MFFL scoring categories, just not computable from aggregate
   career stats):
     - The +3 yardage bonus at 300 pass / 100 rush / 100 rec yards in a
       single game (needs game logs, not season/career totals).
     - QB sacks taken (-1), fumbles lost (-2), 2-point conversions (+2).
     - Kicker distance tiers: MFFL pays 3 pts for 0-39 yd field goals, 4 for
       40-49, and 5 for 50+. Without a made-FG distance breakdown, every
       field goal below is valued at 3 — the league's most common tier —
       so kickers with a lot of long makes are undercounted here. Checked
       and ruled out several other career-FG leaders (Jason Hanson, Harrison
       Butker) — none approach the current top 10 by career points.
     - Defense: points-allowed bands, safety, blocked kick, and the
       distinction between a return TD and a takeaway TD are all part of
       real MFFL DEF scoring but aren't reflected below; sacks/INT/fumble
       recovery/TD (the categories we do have) match the league's real
       per-category values exactly. The New England Patriots (2496 est.
       pts) came within 2 points of cracking the top 5 (Rams, #4, sit at
       2498) — essentially a statistical tie given the precision of the
       inputs here; the Chicago Bears (2458) were also checked and don't
       threaten the cutoff.

   Near-misses worth noting: at WR, Julio Jones holds the #10 spot at 2680.3
   est. pts, with Keenan Allen (2680.1) and Antonio Brown (2655.1) close
   enough behind that the order among these three should be read as a
   near-tie rather than a confident ranking, given the precision of
   publicly available aggregate stats. */

export interface QbLeader {
  rank: number;
  name: string;
  passYds: number;
  passTd: number;
  rushYds: number;
  rushTd: number;
  ints: number;
  fantasyPts: number;
}

export interface RbLeader {
  rank: number;
  name: string;
  rushYds: number;
  rushTd: number;
  rec: number;
  recYds: number;
  totalTd: number;
  fantasyPts: number;
}

export interface ReceiverLeader {
  rank: number;
  name: string;
  rec: number;
  recYds: number;
  recTd: number;
  ypr: number;
  fantasyPts: number;
}

export interface KLeader {
  rank: number;
  name: string;
  fgMade: number;
  fgAtt: number;
  fgPct: number;
  xpMade: number;
  points: number;
  fantasyPts: number;
}

export interface DefLeader {
  rank: number;
  team: string;
  sacks: number;
  ints: number;
  fumbleRec: number;
  defTd: number;
  fantasyPts: number;
}

